{"causes":["The establishment of the Roman Republic in 509 BCE, which marked a significant shift in the balance of power in ancient Rome.","The growing tensions between the Roman patrician class and the emerging plebeian population, which would eventually lead to the establishment of the Roman Republic.","The influence of the Etruscan civilization, which had a profound impact on the development of Roman politics, society, and culture."],"effects":["The eventual rise of Rome as a dominant world power, which would have far-reaching consequences for the development of Western civilization.","The establishment of the Roman Republic's system of governance, which would serve as a model for future forms of democracy.","The growth of Roman influence throughout the Mediterranean region, which would lead to the expansion of Roman culture, trade, and politics."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"publius-postumius-tubertus","sourceName":"Publius Postumius Tubertus","verb":"COLLABORATES_WITH","targetSlug":"marcus-valerius-volusus","targetName":"Marcus Valerius Volusus","context":"Volusus and Tubertus formed a partnership to govern Rome in 505 BC."},{"sourceSlug":"rome","sourceName":"Rome","verb":"CONTAINS","targetSlug":"marcus-valerius-volusus","targetName":"Marcus Valerius Volusus","context":"Volusus was a consul of Rome in 505 BC."},{"sourceSlug":"italy","sourceName":"Italy","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"marcus-valerius-volusus","targetName":"Marcus Valerius Volusus","context":"Volusus was born in Italy around 550 BCE."},{"sourceSlug":"roman-republic","sourceName":"Roman Republic","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"marcus-valerius-volusus","targetName":"Marcus Valerius Volusus","context":"Volusus's leadership marked a significant shift in the Roman Republic's governance."},{"sourceSlug":"roman-consul","sourceName":"Roman Consul","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"marcus-valerius-volusus","targetName":"Marcus Valerius Volusus","context":"Volusus held the office of Roman consul in 505 BC."},{"sourceSlug":"rome's-influence","sourceName":"Rome's Influence","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"marcus-valerius-volusus","targetName":"Marcus Valerius Volusus","context":"Volusus's leadership contributed to the growth of Rome's influence throughout the Mediterranean region."},{"sourceSlug":"roman-military-strategy","sourceName":"Roman Military Strategy","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"marcus-valerius-volusus","targetName":"Marcus Valerius Volusus","context":"Volusus's leadership played a crucial role in shaping Roman military strategy."}],"places":[{"name":"Rome","role":"Capital City"},{"name":"Italy","role":"Birthplace"},{"name":"Etruria","role":"Influence"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Marcus Valerius Volusus","type":"Reference article","slug":"marcus-valerius-volusus"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q673518","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marcus_Valerius_Volusus"],"tags":["Political Leaders","political-systems","geopolitical-linkage","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"","summary":"Marcus Valerius Volusus (fl. c. 500 BCE; consul 505 BCE) was a Roman Republican politician of the early Republic — one of the patrician consuls of the first generation of the Roman Republic following the expulsion of the Tarquin kings (traditional date: 509 BCE). The Valerian gens (clan) was among the most prestigious of the early Republican patriciate: the Valerians were consistently at the forefront of the early Republican aristocracy, and Marcus Valerius Volusus was one of the members of this dynasty who held the consulship in the Republic's first decades.

Marcus Valerius Volusus is recorded by ancient tradition (primarily through Livy and Dionysius of Halicarnassus) as consul in 505 BCE — a period when the early Roman Republic was engaged in the persistent wars against the Sabines, the Volsci, and other neighbouring Latin and Italic peoples that dominated Roman military activity in the late 6th and early 5th centuries BCE. He is also mentioned in some ancient sources in connection with the early Roman tradition of the dictator (the appointment of a single emergency commander in periods of crisis) — one of the key Republican constitutional innovations. The historical reliability of these early Republican records is contested by modern scholarship: the traditional Roman account of the early Republic was transmitted in writing centuries after the events, and the consul fasti (lists of annual magistrates) may contain interpolations and anachronisms.

The Valerian gens connection is significant beyond Marcus Valerius Volusus himself: the Valerians produced multiple consuls and generals in the early Republic, and the 'Lex Valeria' tradition (associated with Publius Valerius Publicola, the Valerians' most famous early Republican hero) — giving citizens the right of appeal (provocatio) against magistrates — was one of the foundational traditions of Roman Republican liberty. Marcus Valerius Volusus's consulship is one entry in the broader Valerian tradition of early Republican leadership.","subjects":["Roman Republic","Early Roman History","Classical Era","Ancient Rome","Republican Rome","Patrician Aristocracy","Consular History","Classical Italy"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"Volusus's leadership played a significant role in shaping the Roman Republic's governance and the eventual rise of Rome as a dominant world power.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
